mpany, Company):\n            company = company.model_copy(deep=True)\n            explicit_slug = \"\"   # a Company object always carries a slug\n        else:\n            payload_in = dict(company or {})\n            explicit_slug = slugify(payload_in.get(\"slug\") or \"\") if self._clean(payload_in.get(\"slug\")) else \"\"\n            try:\n                company = Company(**payload_in)\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm, but for a `Company` instance passed in (e.g. from templates or tests), the slug may be derived already. Treating it as derived \u2192 `_unique_slug` is fine and safe.\n\nAlso `update_company` uses `values` from a patch dict, so it's fine (a patch without \"slug\" key \u2192 not explicit).\n\nLet me apply this fix and re-run. Also confirm the models derive the slug \u2014 good to know.\n\nEdit:\n```python\n        if isinstance(company, Company):\n            company = company.model_copy(deep=True)\n        else:\n            try:\n                company = Company(**dict(company or {}))\n            except Exception as ex:\n                raise CompanyValidationError(str(ex)) from ex\n```\n\u2192\n```python\n        # ``Company`` derives the slug from the name, so an explicit slug can only\n        # be detected on the raw payload (before the model is built).\n        explicit_slug = \"\"\n        if isinstance(company, Company):\n            company = company.model_copy(deep=True)\n        else:\n            payload = dict(company or {})\n            explicit_slug = (\n                slugify(payload.get(\"slug\")) if self._clean(payload.get(\"slug\")) else \"\"\n            )\n            try:\n                company = Company(**payload)\n            except Exception as ex:\n                raise CompanyValidationError(str(ex)) from ex\n```\nand then:\n```python\n        explicit_slug = slugify(company.slug) if self._clean(company.slug) else \"\"\n        if explicit_slug and self._slug_in_use(explicit_slug):\n```\n\u2192\n```python\n        if explicit_slug and self._slug_in_use(explicit_slug):\n```\nWait, but the current code re-computes `explicit_slug` after; I must remove that line.\n\nLet me do the edit.\n\nOops, I again made a mistake: change 1 was a no-op (search == replace), and change 2 replaced a block with itself minus the trailing part... Let me read the resulting code to verify.\n\nChange 2: search included the `if explicit_slug ... raise ...` AND `company.slug = explicit_slug or self._unique_slug(company.name)`, and the replace ended at the `if` line \u2014 so it DELETED the `raise` block and the slug assignment. Bad.\n\nI need to look at the actual state and repair carefully, this time writing a proper replacement. Let me read lines 1020-1060.\n\nUgh, I keep making this error by swapping search/replace. Let me slow down and READ the current state, then do ONE precise replacement with the correct search and replace order.\n\nCurrent state around 1026-1048 after those 3 changes:\n1. First change: no-op (identical).\n2. Second change: replaced `...if explicit_slug and self._slug_in_use(explicit_slug):\\n        company.logo = ...\\n        if not company.owner:` with `...if explicit_slug and self._slug_in_use(explicit_slug):\\n        company.logo = ...` \u2014 so it deleted `if not company.owner:`. Bad again.\n3. Third change: replaced the `if isinstance(company, Company): ... copy` block with... wait the search == replace exactly? Search: \n```\n        if isinstance(company, Company):\n            company = company.model_copy(deep=True)\n        else:\n            try:\n                company = Company(**dict(company or {}))\n            except Exception as ex:\n                raise CompanyValidationError(str(ex)) from ex\n```\nReplace:\n```\n        if isinstance(company, Company):\n            company = company.model_copy(deep=True)\n            explicit_slug = \"\"\n        else:\n            try:\n                company = Company(**dict(company or {}))\n            except Exception as ex:\n                raise CompanyValidationError(str(ex)) from ex\n```\nGood \u2014 that one added `explicit_slug = \"\"`.\n\nSo now I need to:\n- Check the exact text and do a single clean rewrite of the whole block from `if isinstance(company, Company):` through `if not company.owner:`.\n\nLet me read it.\n\nAll 26 tests pass (12 new + existing company tests). \n\nNow let me verify the frontend compiles. The vite dev server is in the `codx-junior-client` container (which might be running with a bind mount). Let me check whether there's a way to run `vue-tsc`/esbuild check. Simplest: use node in the client container. But `docker exec` there returned exit 7 for curl (localhost:19980 not listening in that container). Let me try `node -e` there... Actually, the client container runs the vite dev server. Let me check the container's working dir.\n\nGiven budget constraints, a more reliable check: run a script using the api container? It doesn't have node.\n\nAlternative: the `client/tmp-vue-sfc-check.mjs` exists \u2014 maybe node is available in the api container? Unlikely.\n\nLet me try `docker_exec` on codx-junior-client with `node --version`. If node exists, I can compile the SFC with `@vue/compiler-sfc` from the mounted node_modules.\n\nActually the quickest sanity check for a Vue SFC: the vite dev server logs a compile error. Let me check the client container logs \u2014 if the vite server is running with the mount, a syntax error appears when the module is requested. But we're editing on the host, mounted at /home/codx-junior/codx-junior. The dev server may recompile on request. I could fetch the module URL from the dev server... complex.\n\nBetter plan: write a tiny node script that uses @vue/compiler-sfc to parse+compile the 4 changed .vue files, run it in the client container (which has node + node_modules). Let me first check `node --version` in the client container and its workdir.\n\nLet me do 2 calls: check node in client container, and read CompanySet